{"candidates":[{"claim":"Ownership-based allocation systematically suppresses cross-cutting explanations by creating self-reinforcing epistemic silos: each lineage optimizes internally and never encounters the anomalies that would force it to bridge to a rival's framework.","mechanism":"A lineage that owns a problem inherits not only its prior artifacts but also its theoretical vocabulary, its acceptable move-set, and its unexpressed background commitments. When a new problem is routed to this lineage, the search is constrained by the inherited framing. A cross-cutting explanation—one that requires adopting a rival's primitive terms or background assumptions—cannot even be *formulated* in the owning lineage's vocabulary, much less accepted. The suppression mechanism is not active censorship but combinatorial infeasibility: the search space defined by Lineage A's commitment-neighborhood excludes the region where a Lineage-B vocabulary concept is the key explanatory bridge. Because each success within the lineage further narrows its search heuristic, this exclusion compounds over time (a path-dependence / lock-in mechanism).","counterconditions":["The system possesses a meta-heuristic that periodically dissolves lineage boundaries, reassigning problems to randomly composed multi-lineage coalitions.","The inherited commitment-neighborhood of each lineage is provably a spanning set over all possible explanatory primitives, so the required bridge is always constructible.","A 'cross-cutting' explanation is defined so narrowly that it requires a specific type of concept that only one lineage's vocabulary can express, making the exclusion tautological rather than systematic.","The system's evaluation function explicitly rewards explanations that reuse primitives from rival lineages, counteracting the internal optimization pressure."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_001"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_001","SRC_002","SRC_003"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"analogy":{"disanalogies":["Scientific disciplines are not algorithmically constrained by a program's search vocabulary.","Kuhn's scientific communities lack a central 'harness' adjudicator that explicitly routes problems.","In science, a crisis period is a sociological phenomenon; in an algorithmic system, it must be formalized as a saturation threshold."],"overturn_conditions":["Find that problem similarity within lineages is low, indicating branching is random, not specialization.","Find that the system lacks any saturation detection, yet cross-cutting explanations still emerge spontaneously."],"shared_structure":["Inheritance of a paradigm/commitment-set that constrains the search space for solutions.","Difficulty articulating problems in a rival paradigm's vocabulary.","Problem routing to specialized communities rather than open competition."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["Just as scientific paradigms suppress cross-cutting work by making it illegible until a crisis, ownership allocation suppresses cross-cutting explanations until lineage-specific failure saturates.","The discovery of cross-cutting explanations in a single run should mirror scientific revolutions: occurring only after a measurable build-up of unresolvable anomalies within the owning lineage."]},"claim":"Ownership-based allocation is analogous to Kuhnian normal science: it efficiently exploits existing paradigms but suppresses revolutionary, cross-cutting explanations by routing problems to the very paradigm that is least equipped to see its own anomalies as requiring a paradigm-shift.","mechanism":"The 'normal science' of a lineage involves puzzle-solving within its inherited framework. The framework dictates both what counts as a solution and what counts as a problem. A cross-cutting explanation is essentially a paradigm shift—a re-conceptualization that crosses lineage boundaries. By allocating problems to the lineage that produced the parent, the system ensures that problems are always addressed as 'normal science' for that lineage. The lineage will work diligently on the problem, but it will only produce solutions that are legible within its own paradigm. Cross-cutting explanations, which require seeing the problem through a rival's lens, are suppressed because they fall outside the 'normal science' of the owning lineage.","counterconditions":["The lineages are not true 'paradigms' but merely different starting points in a continuous space, so 'cross-cutting' solutions are just a metric distance away and easily reached.","The system's harness acts as a revolutionary agent, deliberately seeding rival lineages' concepts into the owning lineage's context.","Every lineage's 'normal science' is so broad that it encompasses all rival paradigms as sub-cases, making paradigm shifts unnecessary."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_002"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"claim":"Ownership-based allocation is a greedy optimization strategy that is locally efficient but globally suboptimal. It does not systematically suppress cross-cutting explanations but merely defers their discovery until the marginal efficiency of within-lineage search drops below the cost of cross-lineage transfer.","mechanism":"The cost of formulating a cross-cutting explanation includes the cost of acquiring a rival's vocabulary and the risk of incompatibility. In the early stages of problem-solving, within-lineage search has a low marginal cost because the vocabulary and commitments are already loaded. Ownership allocation greedily exploits this low cost. As the within-lineage search saturates (all local moves exhausted), the marginal cost of further within-lineage work rises. Eventually, this cost exceeds the fixed cost of cross-lineage transfer, at which point a cross-cutting explanation becomes economically rational. Thus, the apparent 'suppression' is just a rational deferral under a cost-of-search model.","counterconditions":["The cost of cross-lineage transfer is infinite or unbounded (e.g., incommensurability), so the deferral never terminates.","The system terminates before the saturation point is reached, so the deferral becomes permanent suppression.","Within-lineage search is non-saturating (infinitely productive), so the cross-over point is never reached."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_003"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"claim":"Ownership-based allocation suppresses cross-cutting explanations by disabling the critical mechanism of re-framing: the ability to see a problem simultaneously through multiple theoretical lenses. By binding a problem to a single owning lineage, the system loses the epistemic diversity needed to triangulate explanations that span domains.","mechanism":"A cross-cutting explanation is often a structural analogy—identifying that a problem in Domain A has the same formal structure as a solved problem in Domain B. This recognition requires simultaneous activation of both domains' frameworks. Ownership allocation serializes and isolates this process: the problem is handed to Domain A, which processes it alone. The mechanism of suppression is the prevention of multi-activation. Without simultaneous representations, the structural mapping required for a cross-cutting analogy is never computed.","counterconditions":["A single lineage's vocabulary is rich enough to express all possible structural forms, making multi-activation unnecessary.","The system has a post-processing step that compares all lineage solutions and synthesizes structural mappings, effectively simulating simultaneous multi-activation.","Structural analogies are not the primary source of cross-cutting explanations; instead, they arise from within-domain deep processing."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_003"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"claim":"Suppression versus efficiency can be distinguished by examining the failure modes of the owning lineage. If it is efficiency, failures will be 'depth-limit' (local optima). If it is suppression, failures will be 'vocab-limit' (un-expressible). In a single run, suppression is evidenced by problems where the owning lineage generates no candidate at all, or only tautological ones, while a rival lineage—given the same problem—immediately produces a non-trivial candidate.","mechanism":"Efficiency implies a rational trade-off: the chosen path (within-lineage) is the most cost-effective, and failure occurs when the problem is too hard for any lineage. Suppression implies an artificial constraint: the chosen path is incapable of expressing the solution, even if the solution is easily expressible in another path. The distinguishing mechanism is the expressibility gap. If the owning lineage fails because it lacks the computational resources to explore its own space, that is efficiency. If it fails because its space does not contain the solution concept, that is suppression.","counterconditions":["Vocab-limit failures are indistinguishable from extreme depth-limit failures where all non-tautological candidates are pruned.","Rival lineages always produce non-trivial candidates for any problem, making the test trivially true and non-discriminating.","The system never allows rival lineages to attempt the same problem, making the counterfactual unobservable in a single run."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_004"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"claim":"To test suppression by counting: count the lineage provenance of all accepted explanatory artifacts (A) and the lineage provenance of all *referenced* primitives in those artifacts (P). Calculate the cross-lineage reference ratio C = P_cross / P_total. If C is consistently zero or near-zero, and the system exhibits a high rate of within-lineage saturation (stagnation) signals, then suppression is occurring. If C is non-zero and proportional to the diversity of active lineages, it is efficiency.","mechanism":"Cross-cutting explanations require the transfer of explanatory primitives across lineage boundaries. If ownership allocation suppresses these explanations, the flow of primitives across boundaries will be choked off. The count C measures this flow. The mechanism distinguishing suppression from efficiency is the presence of stagnation. Under an efficiency model, low cross-lineage flow is compensated by high within-lineage success. Under suppression, low cross-lineage flow co-occurs with within-lineage stagnation (the lineage is stuck but cannot reach outside for help).","counterconditions":["Cross-lineage reference is not the only form of cross-cutting explanation; structural analogies may not require explicit primitive references.","Stagnation signals are not well-defined or are not recorded in the run's artifacts.","The diversity of active lineages is low (e.g., only one), making C trivially zero."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_004"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"scratch_proposal":{"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_003"],"content":"Definition of 'cross-cutting suppression test': In a single reasoning run, select a sample of solved problems P. For each p in P, identify the owning lineage L_owner. Simulate (or observe, if the run already did so) the rival lineages L_rival solving p. If L_rival produces a valid solution that uses a primitive not in L_owner's vocabulary, and L_owner failed or produced a weaker solution, this is a positive instance of suppression. Count: S = number of positive instances. E = number of instances where L_owner succeeded and L_rival failed. Ratio S/(S+E) > threshold (e.g., 0.3) indicates systematic suppression.","possible_next_move":"Search for a run log where problems were broadcast to multiple lineages to compute this ratio empirically.","why_keep_this":"This provides a concrete, countable metric for the suppression vs efficiency distinction, directly addressing the problem's request for 'what to count'."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Is it possible for a lineage to have a 'universal grammar' that contains all rival primitives as derivable theorems, making cross-lineage reference unnecessary in principle, even if inefficient in practice?"}]},"simulation_proposals":[{"declared_assumptions":["Lineage vocabularies are finite sets of discrete primitives.","Problem complexity scales linearly with N.","The cost of cross-lineage transfer is a fixed constant K.","A solution is found if the problem's required primitive is in the searching lineage's vocabulary."],"discriminating_purpose":"To observe whether cross-lineage vocabulary usage collapses to zero (suppression) or stabilizes at a non-zero value (efficiency) as a function of lineage search saturation, under a greedy ownership allocation regime.","hypothesis":"Under ownership-based allocation, the within-lineage success rate will be high for low-complexity problems but will plateau as complexity increases, while the cross-lineage solution rate (where a rival vocabulary is needed) will remain permanently zero, demonstrating systematic suppression rather than efficiency.","interpretation_conditions":["Compare the cross_lineage_solution_rate of the ownership model against a baseline random allocation model.","A suppression effect is identified if ownership_model.cross_lineage_rate < baseline.cross_lineage_rate * 0.2 for problems with complexity > 5.","An efficiency effect is identified if ownership_model.within_lineage_rate > baseline.within_lineage_rate * 1.5 AND ownership_model.cross_lineage_rate >= baseline.cross_lineage_rate * 0.5."],"model_source":"def simulate(inputs, rng):\n    N = inputs['parameters']['problem_complexity']\n    V_size = inputs['parameters']['vocab_size']\n    K_cost = inputs['parameters']['cross_transfer_cost']\n    allocation = inputs['parameters']['allocation_mode']\n    L = inputs['parameters']['num_lineages']\n\n    # Each lineage has a random vocabulary subset\n    lineages = [set(rng.sample(range(V_size), V_size // 2)) for _ in range(L)]\n\n    within_success = 0\n    cross_success = 0\n    total = 100\n\n    for _ in range(total):\n        # Problem requires a random primitive\n        req_prim = rng.randint(0, V_size - 1)\n        if allocation == 'ownership':\n            # Assign to lineage 0 (the 'owner')\n            owner = 0\n            if req_prim in lineages[owner]:\n                within_success += 1\n            else:\n                # Check if any rival could solve it (simulating the counterfactual)\n                for r in range(1, L):\n                    if req_prim in lineages[r]:\n                        cross_success += 1\n                        break\n        else: # random\n            chosen = rng.randint(0, L - 1)\n            if req_prim in lineages[chosen]:\n                if chosen == 0: within_success += 1\n                else: cross_success += 1\n\n    return {\n        'within_lineage_rate': within_success / total,\n        'cross_lineage_rate': cross_success / total,\n        'suppression_index': 1.0 - (cross_success / (total - within_success + 1e-9))\n    }","parameter_definitions":[{"name":"base_run","values_json":"{\"problem_complexity\": 10, \"vocab_size\": 100, \"cross_transfer_cost\": 5, \"allocation_mode\": \"ownership\", \"num_lineages\": 4}"},{"name":"control_random","values_json":"{\"problem_complexity\": 10, \"vocab_size\": 100, \"cross_transfer_cost\": 5, \"allocation_mode\": \"random\", \"num_lineages\": 4}"},{"name":"high_complexity","values_json":"{\"problem_complexity\": 50, \"vocab_size\": 100, \"cross_transfer_cost\": 5, \"allocation_mode\": \"ownership\", \"num_lineages\": 4}"}],"request_identifier":"sim_ownership_suppression_001","requested_observables":["within_lineage_rate","cross_lineage_rate","suppression_index"],"rival_predictions":["The random allocation model will exhibit a cross_lineage_rate of approximately 0.75, while the ownership model will exhibit a cross_lineage_rate of 0.0."],"simulation_mode":"sandboxed_python_v1"}]}